<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Beaujeu</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1400 CE (approx.) · capital Beaujeu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The barony of the Beaujolais hills between Lyon and Mâcon, whose lords founded Villefranche-sur-Saône and served the French crown before it passed to the Bourbons.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaujeu">Beaujeu</a> appear in the 10th century as castellans in the hills west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>, between the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-forez">Forez</a> and the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. Their castle at Beaujeu gave its name to the Beaujolais, and they gradually brought the surrounding parishes under their control.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords built a compact barony and around 1140 founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villefranche-sur-saone">Villefranche-sur-Saône</a>, which became its commercial centre and later capital. Vineyards and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> trade underpinned its economy. Lords such as Humbert V, constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, rose high in royal service, and the family married into the houses of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-forez">Forez</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1400 the last lord, Edward II, childless and in debt, left the Beaujolais to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</a>, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-bourbon">Duchy of Bourbon</a>. After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-bourbon">Bourbon</a> lands were confiscated in 1527, it passed through several holders before ending up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> family.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 950 CE – 1400 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.61%;width:8.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beaujeu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Humbert V of Beaujeu, Edward II of Beaujeu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Franco-Provençal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Beaujolais, Barony of Beaujeu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1175&amp;focus=lordship-of-beaujeu" aria-label="Lordship of Beaujeu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4833 613 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Beaujeu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5153 869L5229 694M5153 869L5078 797"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5078 797h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5153 869h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5153 869h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Beaujeu. <a href="/atlas?year=1175&amp;focus=lordship-of-beaujeu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
